### What does Collections.checkedList do?

Collections.checkedList wraps a List with a type-checking decorator. Every insertion operation (add, set, addAll) verifies that the element is an instance of the declared type. If the check fails, a ClassCastException is thrown immediately at the point of insertion.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class CheckedListDemo {
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WITHOUT checked wrapper: CCE at get time
        List<String> unsafe = new ArrayList<>();
        List rawRef = unsafe;
        rawRef.add(42); // Integer sneaks in silently

        try {
            String s = unsafe.get(0); // CCE HERE, far from the bug
        } catch (ClassCastException e) {
            System.out.println("Unsafe: CCE at get: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // WITH checked wrapper: CCE at add time
        List<String> safe = Collections.checkedList(new ArrayList<>(), String.class);
        List rawSafe = safe;

        try {
            rawSafe.add(42); // CCE HERE, at insertion!
        } catch (ClassCastException e) {
            System.out.println("Safe: CCE at add: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // Correct types pass through
        safe.add("SAV2001");
        safe.add("CUR3001");
        System.out.println("Safe list: " + safe);
    }
}
```

```text
Unsafe: CCE at get: class java.lang.Integer cannot be cast to class java.lang.String
Safe: CCE at add: Attempt to insert class java.lang.Integer element into collection with element type class java.lang.String
Safe list: [SAV2001, CUR3001]
```

### How does checkedMap work?

checkedMap verifies both the key type and the value type on every put operation.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class CheckedMapDemo {
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> balances = Collections.checkedMap(
            new HashMap<>(), String.class, Integer.class
        );

        balances.put("SAV2001", 500000);
        balances.put("CUR3001", 1200000);
        System.out.println("Balances: " + balances);

        // Wrong key type
        Map rawRef = balances;
        try {
            rawRef.put(42, "wrong"); // CCE at put time
        } catch (ClassCastException e) {
            System.out.println("Wrong key: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // Wrong value type
        try {
            rawRef.put("FD4001", "not-a-number"); // CCE at put time
        } catch (ClassCastException e) {
            System.out.println("Wrong value: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Balances: {SAV2001=500000, CUR3001=1200000}
Wrong key: Attempt to insert class java.lang.Integer key into collection with key type class java.lang.String
Wrong value: Attempt to insert class java.lang.String value into collection with value type class java.lang.Integer
```

### When should you use checked collections?

Checked collections are most valuable in three scenarios.

- **Legacy code integration.** When passing a typed collection to a legacy API that uses raw types, wrap it first to catch any type violations immediately.
- **Debugging heap pollution.** When a mysterious ClassCastException occurs at retrieval time, wrap the collection at creation to find where the wrong type is inserted.
- **API boundaries.** When exposing a collection to third-party code or plugins, wrap it to protect against accidental type violations.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class LegacyIntegrationDemo {

    // Simulates a legacy API that uses raw types
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    static void legacyProcess(List accounts) {
        accounts.add("SAV2001");
        accounts.add(42); // bug in legacy code!
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = Collections.checkedList(new ArrayList<>(), String.class);

        try {
            legacyProcess(accounts); // CCE on the add(42) call
        } catch (ClassCastException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught at legacy boundary: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        System.out.println("Accounts: " + accounts);
    }
}
```

```text
Caught at legacy boundary: Attempt to insert class java.lang.Integer element into collection with element type class java.lang.String
Accounts: [SAV2001]
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Wrapping after pollution has already occurred

```java
// WRONG: wrap after data is already polluted
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
List rawRef = list;
rawRef.add(42); // pollution already happened
List<String> checked = Collections.checkedList(list, String.class);
// The 42 is already inside! Checked wrapper only protects future adds.
```

```java
// CORRECT: wrap at creation, before any data enters
List<String> checked = Collections.checkedList(new ArrayList<>(), String.class);
checked.add("SAV2001"); // type-checked
```

#### Mistake: Assuming the checked wrapper protects the underlying collection

```java
// WRONG: accessing the original bypasses the wrapper
List<String> original = new ArrayList<>();
List<String> checked = Collections.checkedList(original, String.class);
List rawOriginal = original;
rawOriginal.add(42); // bypasses the checked wrapper!
System.out.println(checked.get(1)); // CCE at get!
```

```java
// CORRECT: never retain a reference to the underlying collection
List<String> checked = Collections.checkedList(new ArrayList<>(), String.class);
// Only use 'checked', never the underlying list directly
```

### How is this different from generics?

| Aspect | Generics | Checked collections |
|---|---|---|
| When checked | Compile time | Runtime |
| Detection of wrong type | COMPILE ERROR | ClassCastException at add |
| Works with raw types | No (bypassed) | Yes (catches at runtime) |
| Performance cost | None | One instanceof per add |
| Use case | All new code | Legacy integration, debugging |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **checkedNavigableMap and checkedNavigableSet.** Java 8 added checked wrappers for NavigableMap and NavigableSet.
- **No generic array enforcement.** Checked collections cannot prevent generic array creation issues (new List<String>[]) since arrays are reified differently.
- **SortedMap/SortedSet variants.** `Collections.checkedSortedMap` and `checkedSortedSet` maintain sorted semantics while adding type checks.
- **Not a replacement for generics.** Checked collections add a runtime safety net but are not a substitute for proper generic type usage. They are a debugging and migration tool.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What does Collections.checkedList do?" : It wraps a list with runtime type checking on every insertion, throwing ClassCastException at add time instead of get time.
- "When would you use it?" : When passing collections to legacy raw-type code, debugging heap pollution, or protecting API boundaries.
- "Does it have a performance cost?" : Yes, one instanceof check per insertion, but it is negligible for most use cases.
- "Does it protect against heap pollution through the original reference?" : No. You must not retain a reference to the underlying collection.
